1
00:00:00,490 --> 00:00:02,070
Alright, so another trait of a dysfunctional director

2
00:00:02,070 --> 00:00:06,030
is having an overbearing and domineering style that

3
00:00:06,030 --> 00:00:09,190
suppresses dissent or diversity of opinion.

4
00:00:09,750 --> 00:00:12,590
Unfortunately, in certain instances, the chairman is the

5
00:00:12,590 --> 00:00:17,650
one that exhibits this domineering style or failure

6
00:00:17,650 --> 00:00:19,890
to want to accept dissenting views on the

7
00:00:19,890 --> 00:00:20,150
board.

8
00:00:20,810 --> 00:00:22,930
Another feature of a dysfunctional director is that

9
00:00:22,930 --> 00:00:24,690
who wants to be the CEO always second

10
00:00:24,690 --> 00:00:27,370
-guessing or trying to micromanage the CEO.

11
00:00:28,170 --> 00:00:30,970
A director that breaches confidentiality before the meeting

12
00:00:30,970 --> 00:00:32,830
is over, he or she is sending text

13
00:00:32,830 --> 00:00:35,630
messages to people outside of the meeting who

14
00:00:35,630 --> 00:00:39,910
doesn't keep the board's confidence and some people

15
00:00:39,910 --> 00:00:42,550
that we consider to be board veterans, they've

16
00:00:42,550 --> 00:00:44,070
been on the boards for so many years

17
00:00:44,070 --> 00:00:45,550
and they feel that they know if they've

18
00:00:45,550 --> 00:00:47,250
done it, they have the t-shirt.

19
00:00:48,150 --> 00:00:49,610
So what does the board then do having

20
00:00:49,610 --> 00:00:51,850
identified a dysfunctional director?

21
00:00:51,850 --> 00:00:53,670
First of all, before the board can come

22
00:00:53,670 --> 00:00:56,890
to the conclusion that the director is dysfunctional,

23
00:00:57,630 --> 00:01:00,770
it's best that this conclusion is arrived at

24
00:01:00,770 --> 00:01:02,910
in an objective manner and one of the

25
00:01:02,910 --> 00:01:04,750
ways of doing that is by the board

26
00:01:04,750 --> 00:01:05,290
evaluation.

27
00:01:05,870 --> 00:01:10,250
So periodically, the board would have an evaluation

28
00:01:10,250 --> 00:01:13,470
of its performance and that evaluation includes a

29
00:01:13,470 --> 00:01:17,990
peer review of each director's performance and if

30
00:01:17,990 --> 00:01:20,610
the results of the peer review indicates that

31
00:01:20,610 --> 00:01:23,250
a particular director has been adjudged by his

32
00:01:23,250 --> 00:01:27,090
peers as being dysfunctional, this is an objective

33
00:01:27,090 --> 00:01:31,370
test and so the chair of the board

34
00:01:31,370 --> 00:01:34,170
is expected to have a conversation with such

35
00:01:34,170 --> 00:01:37,230
a director, I mean making them aware of

36
00:01:37,230 --> 00:01:39,710
how they've been assessed or viewed by their

37
00:01:39,710 --> 00:01:45,210
peers and oftentimes that conversation achieves the purpose

38
00:01:45,210 --> 00:01:46,230
for which it was intended.

39
00:01:46,790 --> 00:01:49,770
At other times it doesn't, some training or

40
00:01:49,770 --> 00:01:52,470
coaching might be required to help the director

41
00:01:52,470 --> 00:01:56,270
bridge the gaps that have been identified because

42
00:01:56,270 --> 00:01:58,370
sometimes people are just not aware that they

43
00:01:58,370 --> 00:01:59,630
have such deficiencies.

44
00:02:00,470 --> 00:02:03,290
The extreme or the most stringent action is

45
00:02:03,290 --> 00:02:05,470
to have the person removed from the board.

46
00:02:05,810 --> 00:02:07,449
We'll recall that I had said earlier that

47
00:02:07,449 --> 00:02:11,890
removal from the is no longer a palatable

48
00:02:11,890 --> 00:02:16,530
option because it prevents the director from being

49
00:02:16,530 --> 00:02:17,810
able to sit on another board.

50
00:02:17,950 --> 00:02:20,210
So what would then happen is for the

51
00:02:20,210 --> 00:02:23,230
board to encourage such a director to resign.

52
00:02:23,850 --> 00:02:25,710
The question can be asked that what if

53
00:02:25,710 --> 00:02:30,390
this dysfunctional director is a representative of a

54
00:02:30,390 --> 00:02:34,650
significant shareholder but they are themselves representing their

55
00:02:34,650 --> 00:02:35,710
shareholder on the board.

56
00:02:36,250 --> 00:02:39,470
Really and truly it depends on how effective

57
00:02:39,470 --> 00:02:40,470
that board is.

58
00:02:40,570 --> 00:02:42,470
If it's truly a board that is effective

59
00:02:42,470 --> 00:02:45,610
then regardless of whether or not the person

60
00:02:45,610 --> 00:02:49,070
is representing a significant shareholder they should be

61
00:02:49,070 --> 00:02:50,870
able to show the results of the board

62
00:02:50,870 --> 00:02:54,670
evaluation and have the significant shareholder replace this

63
00:02:54,670 --> 00:02:56,270
person on the board.

64
00:02:56,350 --> 00:02:58,470
So you're not taking away their right to

65
00:02:58,470 --> 00:03:01,490
appoint a representative on the board but you're

66
00:03:01,490 --> 00:03:04,910
asking them to appoint a person or an

67
00:03:04,910 --> 00:03:07,910
individual that will add value rather than take

68
00:03:07,910 --> 00:03:09,610
away value from the board.
